# Code owners for the CI enforcement surface.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
# The `RLS Boundaries (local)` HTTP cron gate and the Mobile Rollout Gate both run their
# enforcement logic from the pull request's BASE revision rather than from the pull request
# itself. That base-pinning removes some bypasses, but it cannot remove the general one: a pull
# request that can change what CI executes can weaken or delete any check CI runs. Reviews of
# PR #1174 demonstrated several working bypasses, and each one needed an edit to a file matched
# below.
#
# So owner review of these paths -- not any assertion inside a gate script -- is the actual
# boundary. Everything the gates do themselves is defence in depth behind this file.
#
# WHAT HAS TO BE COVERED, AND WHY IT IS MORE THAN `.github/`
# Workflow files are the obvious half. The other half is everything a workflow step already
# executes on its own, because reaching those needs NO workflow edit at all:
#
#   * The root `package.json`'s `prepare` script (`husky || true`) runs during every
#     `pnpm install --frozen-lockfile`, which the `RLS Boundaries (local)` job does about ten
#     steps before it runs the base-pinned gate. A `prepare` that also drops a `git` or `psql`
#     shim onto $GITHUB_PATH defeats the pinning while the log still prints "enforcement logic
#     is pinned." -- demonstrated during review. `packageManager` is pnpm@10.23.0, which blocks
#     DEPENDENCY lifecycle scripts by default, so the root manifest's own `prepare` is the live
#     route; pnpm-workspace.yaml's `allowBuilds` is the switch that would re-open the dependency
#     one.
#   * The lockfile and .npmrc decide what that install actually fetches and from where.
#   * Workflow-invoked scripts live in more places than scripts/ci: the migration security gate
#     is scripts/check-migration-security.mjs and the forbidden-pattern scan is
#     scripts/forbidden-pattern-scan.sh, so the whole of scripts/ is covered rather than just
#     the base-pinned subdirectory.
#
# IMPORTANT: THIS FILE ALONE DOES NOT ENFORCE ANYTHING.
# GitHub only requires a code owner's approval when a ruleset or classic branch protection on
# the target branch turns on "Require review from Code Owners". At the time this file was added
# the repository's only ruleset was non_fast_forward + deletion, and `dev` had no classic
# protection, so CODEOWNERS was advisory: it requested owner review and nothing blocked a merge
# without it. Turning the requirement on for `dev` and `main` is a repository-settings change.
# Until that is done, treat the paths below as convention.

# Workflow definitions, action pins, permissions blocks, and this file.
/.github/ @billmal071

# Every script a workflow executes -- the base-pinned gates in scripts/ci, and the other
# enforcement scripts (check-migration-security.mjs, forbidden-pattern-scan.sh) beside them.
/scripts/ @billmal071

# The install surface of the job that runs the gates: the `prepare` hook that runs inside
# `pnpm install`, what husky wires up, what the install resolves, and the dependency
# build-script allow-list.
/package.json @billmal071
/pnpm-lock.yaml @billmal071
/pnpm-workspace.yaml @billmal071
/.npmrc @billmal071
/.husky/ @billmal071
